I'll review this markdown slice read-only against the pinned upstream oracle, the round-2 inventory, and the port decisions, and report only new findings.Round 2 already filed many of these files. I'll check what changed at this commit and only look for issues the inventory did not close.The round-2 fixes are in the tree. I’ll read the new serializer and scanner paths for regressions the earlier inventory did not cover.The review is still open on the unread block files and the two unfiled stringify cases. I’ll read those against the oracle and only then write findings.The two stringify holes are in the source. Next I’ll finish the unread block parsers and check whether the ledger already records either hole.The stringify contract is reparse-equivalence. I’ll finish the unread parsers before deciding which holes are new.### fable-3-1
- file: scratchpad/effected/markdown/internal/stringify.ts:1013
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, and §14 (`upstream-bug`); render-equivalence in `scratchpad/effected/markdown/Markdown.ts:508`; fenced opener `reCodeFence` in `scratchpad/effected/markdown/internal/blocks/fencedCode.ts:13` and closing rule in `scratchpad/effected/markdown/internal/blocks/code.ts:98`.   evidence: `encodeCodeInfo` encodes `&`, `\`, `` ` ``, and whitespace, then `serializeCode` concatenates that string onto the fence at line 1042. A tilde fence does not encode `~`. `Code` `{ fenceChar: "~", lang: "~", value: "x\n" }` therefore emits `~~~~\nx\n~~~`. `^~{3,}` takes all four opening tildes, so the info string is empty, and the three-tilde closer is shorter than the opener, so it stays content. Reparse has no language and value `x\n~~~\n`. `~~x` likewise becomes a five-tilde opener whose info is `x`. This is the remaining hole beside the recorded backtick repair (round-2 req-37); the ledger’s info-string deviation does not mention tildes. `&#126;` is a decimal reference `decodeEntity` accepts, and `finalizeFenced` unescapes after the fence match, so one encoded leading tilde is enough.
- failure: A tilde-fenced code node whose language starts with `~` does not reparse. The language is absorbed into the opening fence and, when that fence grows past the closer, the closing fence becomes code content.
- fix: After the existing replacements, encode one leading `~` as `&#126;` when the fence character is `~`. Add a round-trip for `lang: "~"` and `lang: "~~x"` with `fenceChar: "~"`.

### fable-3-2
- file: scratchpad/effected/markdown/internal/stringify.ts:556
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, and §14 (`upstream-bug`); render-equivalence in `Markdown.ts:508`; `Link.url` / `Image.url` / `Definition.url` are `S.String` (`MarkdownNode.ts:585`, `:723`, `:953`); `reLinkDestinationBraces` excludes raw newline and the failed `<` peek returns no destination (`references.ts:45`, `:255`).   evidence: `forcesPointy` is true for every code point `<= 0x20`, so a URL `a\nb` is emitted as `<a\nb>` after only `&` and `<>\` escaping. `preprocessLines` treats LF and CR as terminators, and the paragraph subject still contains a newline. The braced-destination pattern rejects that newline, the following character is `<`, and `parseLinkDestination` returns no destination. The same path serves inline links, images, and definitions (call sites at lines 730, 735, and 1066). Space, tab, VT, and FF stay inside the class and are not this failure. NUL is replaced by U+FFFD before the match, and `&#0;` cannot restore NUL, so it is not part of the fix. Upstream’s `destination` has the same raw line ending; the lab’s `&amp;` repair did not cover it.
- failure: A schema-valid link, image, or definition whose URL contains LF or CR stringifies to source that reparses as ordinary text. The destination, and the link itself, are lost.
- fix: In the pointy branch, after the `&` → `&amp;` replacement, encode `\n` and `\r` as `&#10;` and `&#13;`. Add link, image, and definition round-trips for both.

### fable-3-3
- file: scratchpad/effected/markdown/internal/stringify.ts:578
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, and §14 (`upstream-bug`); render-equivalence in `Markdown.ts:508`; paragraph continuation ends on a blank line (`paragraph.ts:41`).   evidence: `titleSuffix` leaves newlines raw inside the quotes. One newline stays inside a paragraph, and `reLinkTitle` (`references.ts:36`) allows it. Two consecutive newlines do not: `[x](http://u "a\n\nb")` emits a blank line, the block pass ends the paragraph at `[x](http://u "a`, and the title never closes. `escapeText` already encodes a newline adjacent to another newline as `&#10;` (line 254). `&#10;` decodes back to a newline, so encoding both line endings in the title is representable.
- failure: A link, image, or definition title that contains a blank line (LF LF, or CR that preprocess turns into a line break beside another break) drops the link on reparse.
- fix: In `titleSuffix`, encode `\n` and `\r` as `&#10;` and `&#13;` after the `&` encoding. Add a title round-trip that contains `\n\n`.

